WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
What heavy delegation does to skill.

2
00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:12.653
In January 2026, Anthropic researchers Judy Hanwen Shen and Alex Tamkin published a randomized controlled trial on exactly this question.

3
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:23.003
Fifty-two mostly junior software engineers, each with more than a year of weekly Python use, learned Trio, an asynchronous programming library none of them had used.

4
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:27.123
Half had an AI assistant in a sidebar; half coded by hand.

5
00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:31.136
Afterwards, everyone took a quiz on the concepts they’d just used.

6
00:00:31.416 --> 00:00:43.192
The AI group averaged 50% on the quiz; the hand-coding group averaged 67%, a difference the authors describe as nearly two letter grades, and statistically significant.

7
00:00:43.472 --> 00:00:45.989
The largest gap was on debugging questions.

8
00:00:46.269 --> 00:00:52.200
The AI group finished about two minutes faster, but that difference was not statistically significant.

9
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.085
The more useful finding is about how people used the assistant.

10
00:00:56.365 --> 00:00:59.544
The authors grouped participants by interaction pattern.

11
00:00:59.824 --> 00:01:10.683
The lowest scorers, averaging under 40%, delegated code generation to the AI, increasingly relied on it, or used it to debug iteratively without understanding.

12
00:01:10.963 --> 00:01:23.187
The highest scorers, at 65% or more, generated code and then worked to understand it, asked for explanations alongside code, or used the AI only for conceptual questions.

13
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:31.403
The authors’ interpretation is that cognitive effort, including getting painfully stuck, is likely important for building mastery.

14
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:47.299
The limits are real and the authors state them: a small sample, one library, comprehension measured immediately rather than over months, and a chat-style assistant rather than an agentic tool like Claude Code, which they expect could have larger effects.

15
00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:51.312
Put it next to the field evidence and a coherent picture emerges.

16
00:01:51.592 --> 00:02:05.032
Cui and colleagues found the largest output gains among less experienced developers; this study suggests those same developers are the ones most at risk of not building the skills they’ll need to oversee the output.

17
00:02:05.312 --> 00:02:08.427
Both can be true, and a team lead should plan for both.
